                    <li>
                      <strong>From token ID to frequency.</strong> We first train a BPE tokenizer on an open-source corpus (e.g., Pile, C4) and count the frequency of each token from the resulting vocabulary. 
                      After obtaining all frequency–ID pairs, 
                      we apply a logarithmic transformation to both frequency and token ID, 
                      converting the inverse proportionality described by Zipf's law into a linear relationship.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Empirical estimates of upper bound, median, and lower bound. </strong>
                      Inspired by quantile regression, which captures the distributional trends of extreme values, 
                      we fit the empirical upper and lower bounds by applying asymmetric penalty weights to data points.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Theoretical supremum and infimum.</strong> Since each new token is constructed from existing tokens in the vocabulary,
                      we argue that the frequency of any token cannot exceed the minimum frequency among all of its constituent subtokens, 
                      nor can it be lower than the maximum frequency among all tokens that contain it as a subtoken. 
                      Such a relationship is formally described by the following equation:
                    </li>
                  </ul>
